[1] In 1858, Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux won a competition to design a large public park in the middle of {{1|Manhattan, their}} plan, which they called the Greensward Plan, imagined meadows, woods, and lakes where swamps and rocky outcrops then stood.

[2] Building the park took years of labor. [3] Workers blasted rock, moved earth, and planted {{2|many thousands of trees}}. [4] {{3|Although}} the finished park looks as if nature had made it, almost every hill and pond was designed. [5] The designers {{4|also tucked roads for crosstown traffic}} below the level of the park, so that carriages could cross it without disturbing visitors. {{5|}}

[6] The park's creation also had a cost that was long overlooked. [7] To clear the land, the city used its power of eminent domain to take property from the people who lived there, including the roughly 225 residents of Seneca Village, a community that was about two-thirds African American and in which more than half of the Black {{6|household's}} owned their land. [8] The village's {{7|churches, homes, and school, were}} demolished.

[9] Today, Central Park {{8|draws}} millions of visitors each year. [10] {{9|Recent historians have worked to make sure that its full history, including Seneca Village, is remembered.}}
